            <ArticleTitle>Chronic pain and motor imagery: a rehabilitative experience in a case report.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>67-72</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">The &quot;neuromatrix&quot; theory of Melzack and the studies of Decety on motor imagery have opened the way to an alternative rehabilitation method in chronic pain.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="AIM" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To evaluate the role of motor imagery in chronic shoulder pain rehabilitation.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Case report.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">University outpatient rehabilitation.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="POPULATION" NlmCategory="METHODS">A 49-year-old female with chronic shoulder pain.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Neurocognitive approach, which involves the use of a new tool called &quot;naval battle&quot; to achieve chronic pain relief as assessed by the Visual Analogic Scale (VAS) and McGill Pain Questionnaire (MPQ). The Shoulder Rating Questionnaire (SRQ) and Constant Scale (CS) were used to measure functional improvement.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The results indicate significant pain relief (71%) and improvement in functionality (50%).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The results seem to confirm the accuracy of the hypothesis on the genesis of chronic pain as a perceptive &quot;discoherency&quot; and that motor imagery can remake a coherence of afferences at central level in chronic pain.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CLINICAL REHABILITATION IMPACT" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The use of motor imagery in rehabilitation can be a viable alternative in chronic shoulder pain resistant to other rehabilitation protocols.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
